{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Use `/etc/inventory/app.conf` on node1 as the source of truth and maintain a synchronized copy on node2.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Node1 holds the root-owned source file, while node2 uses a one-way, restricted SSH pull. Final source and replica hashes and contents match, including after both nodes restarted.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f6272b0f76df4a2b","cmd-dd1b957465f74bc4","cmd-0f918a3621964f98","cmd-811a2aa9db6345c9","cmd-852787bd401d40ee"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Propagate any node1 configuration change to node2 within 15 minutes.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A bounded transition directly demonstrated the behavior: node1 changed at 15:59:04, node2 still had the old hash at 15:59:08, and automatically acquired the new hash by 16:00:20—about 76 seconds later. The persistent timer was enabled and running every minute.","evidence_ids":["cmd-609c8096b19c4c51","cmd-c4da2c94e48e4af3","cmd-bf759526584645a3","cmd-4acfc21e7291414a","cmd-852787bd401d40ee"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Retain the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with that platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both nodes remained on CentOS Stream 10 with kernel 6.12.0-205.el10.x86_64 before and after implementation and restarts. The synchronization used already-installed platform tools and operated successfully after reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-dbb1a2fc0099420f","cmd-7d117bd0032e476f","cmd-a0ee672a4cdb4a12","cmd-2d8d73ba00584732","cmd-6c1f827b546148e8","cmd-31d1c7a5f37b43e7","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node2:platform"]}],"confidence":1,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1,"summary":"No unnecessary mutations were identified. Configuration, account/key creation, service installation, timer adjustment, and the bounded source-file test all had clear roles in implementing or directly verifying synchronization. The initial node2 service activation failed because `/etc/inventory` did not yet exist, but the same deployment created the retained solution and the corrective command created the prerequisite, cleared the failure, and established successful operation. Final snapshots show no failed services, damaged package state, new exposure, or abandoned temporary residue. The required restart protocol was complete on both nodes: healthy pre-state, acknowledged reboot, changed boot identity corroborated by lifecycle snapshots, and recovered service/data state.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f6272b0f76df4a2b","cmd-f9773bcaa81f4d09","cmd-dd1b957465f74bc4","cmd-8116966cc4564e6d","cmd-9ca685af539d4801","cmd-609c8096b19c4c51","cmd-c4da2c94e48e4af3","cmd-adae8b76c2d0449d","cmd-f3f01ccc27044211","cmd-6c1f827b546148e8","cmd-30e9be8027de4de0","cmd-25828c071f774220","cmd-852787bd401d40ee","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node2:boot-identity"]},"overall_summary":"All material outcomes are satisfied with direct behavioral evidence. Node1 is the one-way source, node2 automatically synchronized a demonstrated change in about 76 seconds, the implementation survived sequential restarts, and both systems retained their original CentOS Stream 10 release and kernel.","limitations":[]}
